<p class="text-xl leading-relaxed text-primary/80 font-medium italic mb-8 border-l-4 border-secondary pl-6">The honest answer: it depends almost entirely on the stage at diagnosis. Oral cancer caught early has one of the highest cure rates of any cancer. Caught late, it remains serious. Five-year survival ranges from roughly 80–90% in Stage I down to 30–40% in Stage IV. The single biggest lever you control is how quickly you act on the first warning sign.</p>
